{"source":"def investigate(context, acquire):
    table = {}
    high = 0
    saved = None
    for action in context['actions']:
        op = action['op']
        if op == 'put':
            ident = action['id']
            table[ident] = True
            if ident > high:
                high = ident
        elif op == 'delete':
            table.pop(action['id'], None)
        elif op == 'begin':
            saved = (dict(table), high)
        elif op == 'commit':
            saved = None
        elif op == 'rollback':
            table, high = saved
            saved = None
    return high + 1
","scope":"Model SQLite INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT next-row allocation as max(explicitly inserted ID, including IDs later deleted) + 1. BEGIN snapshots both the row set and the high-water mark; ROLLBACK restores both; COMMIT discards the snapshot. Deletes never lower the high-water mark, and an open transaction at the end does not change the committed measurement.","change":"No behavioral change was required. The supplied program already matches all eight native probes: deleting the maximum row leaves the next ID advanced (1,2 -> 3; 1,3 -> 4; 1,1000 -> 1001; 1000 deleted -> 1001), rollback discards transactional inserts and restores the prior high-water mark (put 1, begin, put 1000, rollback, put 2 -> 3), and commit of a deletion or rollback of a deletion preserves the prior maximum (begin/put 1/delete 1/commit/put 2 -> 3; put 1/begin/delete 1/rollback/put 2 -> 3; put 1/begin/put 2/delete 1/rollback/put 3 -> 4)."}
